dArkOS RG52 Mini - NPU UPSCALE: third-party components
=======================================================

NPU UPSCALE enlarges an emulator's native frame with a small neural network on the RK3562's
NPU, inside dArkOS's Vulkan rotate layer (VK_LAYER_DARKOS_rotate), and for PS1 and the 16-bit
consoles (Mega Drive, SNES, NES, PC Engine, Neo Geo) in RetroArch (32-bit and 64-bit), which
draws with GLES, in libnpusr_gles.so (dArkOS's own, npusr-gles/ in the dArkOS sources). It uses:

1. librknnrt.so - Rockchip RKNN runtime 2.5.0
   Rockchip Electronics Co., Ltd.
   Under the License Agreement for RKNN Software Development Kit Package (its full text is
   /usr/share/doc/librknnrt/LICENSE, npusr/LICENSE.rknn in the dArkOS sources, from the same
   release): for applications compatible with Rockchip's products (1.1), its notices kept
   (2.1(b)). dArkOS uses it only on the RK3562 of the RG52 Mini. Unmodified, as
   published in https://github.com/airockchip/rknn-toolkit2 at the tag v2.5.0,
   rknpu2/runtime/Linux/librknn_api/aarch64/librknnrt.so
   sha256 4b6cc575bedf8952b13db4b1190fa3b3d2ac213f00be774ff6ccd19d651970c0
   Installed as /usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/librknnrt.so; loaded by the layer, or by the 64-bit
   libnpusr_gles.so, only when a game starts with NPU UPSCALE on.
   The same release's 32-bit one, rknpu2/runtime/Linux/librknn_api/armhf/librknnrt.so
   sha256 267193f5f32f01f6e9137072c67ca737d57528257f1a63ce4d65e3698369ffa3
   is installed unmodified as /usr/lib/arm-linux-gnueabihf/librknnrt.so, under the same
   license; loaded by the 32-bit libnpusr_gles.so only when a game starts with NPU UPSCALE on.

2. rknn_api.h - the runtime's header, from the same release and under the same license
   (vk-rotate-layer/rknn_api.h in the dArkOS sources; LICENSE.rknn beside it). libnpusr_gles.so
   is built with it too.

3. ESPCN x2 and x3, TF-ESPCN by Fanny Monori, https://github.com/fannymonori/TF-ESPCN (the
   models OpenCV's dnn_superres module uses), Apache License 2.0 (LICENSE-TF-ESPCN, installed
   beside this file). The teacher the PSP's x2 model (item 4) learned from: it was trained to
   give what ESPCN gives (item 5's models have no teacher). ESPCN_x2.pb and ESPCN_x3.pb
   (TF-ESPCN's export/ files) are kept in npusr/models/ in the dArkOS sources, with
   make_espcn.py, which makes ESPCN itself into a model for the NPU (its first layer rebuilt
   as five rows; NPU UPSCALE ran on it before items 4 and 5, and the layer still takes such
   a model).

4. The PSP's model with PPSSPP's integer scale, /usr/share/darkos/npusr/espcn_x2_480x272.rknn
   (x2 from 480x272; the file name the layer looks for - the network is not ESPCN). Made for
   dArkOS in 2026. A space-to-depth network: 4 luma channels of a 2x2 cell in, 3x3
   convolutions of 32 channels (7 of them, 52 048 weights), 2.4 times faster than ESPCN on
   the NPU. Its weights started from a bicubic interpolation written into the network plus
   random noise (seed 0) - no other network's weights - and were trained to give what ESPCN
   (item 3) gives on the same inputs: screenshots of PSP and 3DS games taken on the console;
   scikit-image's sample photographs (skimage.data: astronaut, coffee, chelsea, rocket,
   camera, coins, moon, page, text - public domain or CC0 by their notes there; moon and page
   have no note of their own and come with scikit-image under its BSD-3-Clause license);
   shapes, text and textures the training script draws. No image dataset. Quantised to INT8
   with rknn-toolkit2 2.5.0, calibrated on 15 PSP screenshots. In the dArkOS sources:
   npusr/models/s2d_distill.py trains it, s2d_rknn.py makes the .rknn file from its weights
   (s2d_x2_v7.pt, kept there: training on a GPU is not bit-exact). The screenshots are not in
   the sources. (s2d_x3_b7.pt beside it is the x3 network of this kind that the image had
   until 2026-11-25; it is not installed.)
   sha256 d2cf6854545702c7331b3747527170047894feba658a6bf1c06a49488fa88bbc  espcn_x2_480x272.rknn

5. The x3 and x4 models, made for dArkOS by its AI-SR session (2026-11-25), in
   /usr/share/darkos/npusr/: espcn_x3_400x240.rknn (the 3DS), espcn_x3_480x272.rknn (the PSP)
   and x3_<W>x<H>.rknn (PS1 and the 16-bit consoles: 256, 320 and 368 by 240 and by 224, and
   304x224 for Neo Geo) - one x3 network made into a model for each frame size - and
   espcn_x4_400x240.rknn (the 3DS on a 1920x1080 TV), an x4 network. The file names are those
   the layer and libnpusr_gles.so look for; the networks are not ESPCN.
   Space-to-depth networks: 4 luma channels of a 2x2 cell in, a 3x3 convolution 4 -> 32
   channels, 7 (x3) or 9 (x4) of 32 -> 32 with ReLU, one 32 -> (2S)^2, clipped to [0,1].
   Their weights started from a bicubic interpolation written into the network - no other
   network's weights - and were trained without a teacher on true high-resolution targets:
   the input a point sample of a larger picture (as a game draws it, no anti-aliasing), the
   target that picture averaged down. The pictures:
   - shapes, text, outlines, triangulations, gradients, textures and pixel patterns the data
     script draws;
   - 224 screenshots of free games (23 more held out for checking): 244 of the 247 from the
     games' own Flathub (appstream) metadata, 3 from Wikimedia Commons. Only games with free
     code and free data; their licences are GPL, LGPL, BSD, MIT, Apache, Zlib, ISC,
     Artistic, GFDL, OFL and CC BY / CC BY-SA mixes, none non-commercial. npusr/models/gt/freeshots-manifest.json in
     the dArkOS sources lists every file: the game, the licence (the project's; for the
     Commons files the file's own and its author), the page and URL, the sha256;
   - frames of the owner's own games taken on the console: Super Mario 3D Land rendered at
     1x, 3x and 4x (pairs of the same moment), native PSP, 3DS, PS1, N64, Saturn and 16-bit
     frames;
   - scikit-image's sample photographs (as in item 4) and procedural textures.
   12% of the inputs got PS1-style dithering (the target clean). Trained in two stages, the
   second with quantisation noise; quantised to INT8 (w8a8) with rknn-toolkit2 2.5.0,
   calibrated on 47 luma frames ("calib-mix": frames of the owner's games as above, and
   sheets of the drawn and screenshot inputs). The owner's game frames are not in the
   sources; npusr/models/gt/calib-mix.SHA256SUMS lists each calibration frame's sha256 (the
   list's own: e14a6eab57ad4f26a99483e68294bd38efc878fa75b30377992a7a3fdbb1757f).
   In the dArkOS sources: npusr/models/gt/ - fetch_flathub.py and fetch_freeshots.py fetch the
   screenshots, sr_data.py builds the training data, mk_calib_mix.py the calibration frames,
   s2d_gt.py trains (with the AI-SR session's paths); npusr/models/s2d_rknn.py makes each
   .rknn from the weights kept beside it (training on a GPU is not bit-exact; from these weights
   and the same calibration frames all ten came out byte for byte on 2026-11-25, with torch
   2.11, onnx 1.23 and OpenCV 4.14 for its onnx step and rknn-toolkit2 2.5.0 for the rknn one):
